true_positives.matched_candidate:
Case-sensitive comparison against existing attendee emails allows duplicates

The dedupe/blacklist filter compares emails with strict equality:

```ts
const uniqueGuests = guests.filter(
    (guest) =>
      !booking.attendees.some((attendee) => guest === attendee.email) &&
      !blacklistedGuestEmails.includes(guest)
);
```

Email addresses are case-insensitive per RFC 5321. Submitting `Test@Example.com` while an existing attendee is `test@example.com` will not be filtered out, so a duplicate attendee row is created (or causes a unique-constraint failure, surfaced to the user). Both the dedupe check and the blacklist comparison must lowercase values first; same applies to `newGuests.includes(attendee.email)` in `sendAddGuestsEmails` which controls whether new guests get the original 'scheduled' email vs the 'guest added' email.

Newly added guests receive the original 'meeting scheduled' confirmation instead of an 'added to meeting' notification

```ts
...calendarEvent.attendees.map((attendee) => {
  if (newGuests.includes(attendee.email)) {
    return sendEmail(() => new AttendeeScheduledEmail(calendarEvent, attendee));
  } else {
    return sendEmail(() => new AttendeeAddGuestsEmail(calendarEvent, attendee));
  }
})
```

`AttendeeScheduledEmail` is the first-touch booking confirmation containing reschedule/cancel links and language framing as if the recipient originally booked it. Newly added guests did not book anything; sending them this template is misleading and may confuse them about whether they own the booking. They should receive `AttendeeAddGuestsEmail` (or a dedicated 'you have been added' template). Additionally, every existing attendee also receives a duplicate `AttendeeAddGuestsEmail` with `icalEvent` `method: REQUEST`, which will trigger another 'invitation' notification in their calendar.

false_positives.candidate:
Calendar update uses the requesting user's credentials instead of the booking organizer's

The handler instantiates the EventManager with the calling session user and their credentials:

```ts
const credentials = await getUsersCredentials(ctx.user);
const eventManager = new EventManager({ ...user, credentials: [...credentials] });
await eventManager.updateCalendarAttendees(evt, booking);
```

When a team admin or attendee (or anyone authorized by the buggy permission check) adds guests, `user` is the requester, not `booking.userId` (the organizer). `updateCalendarAttendees` will then use the requester's calendar integrations to push the updated invite, which can fail, target the wrong calendar, or succeed only partially. Credentials must come from `booking.userId` (or whichever organizer owns the destination calendar).

Email-sending failure is silently logged, but the mutation still returns success

```ts
try {
  await sendAddGuestsEmails(evt, guests);
} catch (err) {
  console.log("Error sending AddGuestsEmails");
}
```

The exception is dropped (no `logger.error`, no context, no rethrow). The handler still returns `{ message: "Guests added" }`, so the client toasts success and the user assumes guests were notified. At minimum, use `logger.error` with the error and rethrow or return a structured failure so the UI can surface it.

forEach(async ...) drops recurring calendar deletion promises

Inside the recurring-cancellation branch the code does:

  bookingToDelete.user.credentials
    .filter((credential) => credential.type.endsWith("_calendar"))
    .forEach(async (credential) => {
      const calendar = await getCalendar(credential);
      for (const updBooking of updatedBookings) {
        ...
        const deletedEvent = await calendar?.deleteEvent(uid, evt, externalCalendarId);
        apiDeletes.push(deletedEvent);
      }
    });

`forEach` does not await its async callback, so the outer flow continues and `Promise.all(prismaPromises.concat(apiDeletes))` runs before the per-credential async loops have pushed their `deletedEvent` Promises (or worse, after a partial race). For recurring bookings this means many calendar deletion calls are effectively dropped or not waited for, leaving the previous time slot still booked on the user's external calendar. Use `for...of` (matching the non-recurring branch a few lines below) or `Promise.all(calendarCredentials.map(...))`.

getCalendarCredentials returns Promise<Calendar> in a sync API

`getCalendarCredentials` is still declared as a synchronous function but internally calls the now-async `getCalendar(credential)` without `await`:

  const calendar = getCalendar(credential);
  return app.variant === "calendar" ? [{ integration: app, credential, calendar }] : [];

So each returned entry's `calendar` field is now `Promise<Calendar | null>` rather than `Calendar | null`. The only caller shown in the diff (`getConnectedCalendars`) was patched to do `const calendar = await item.calendar;`, but the function itself remains a synchronous leaky abstraction. Any other consumer of `getCalendarCredentials` will silently receive a Promise where it expects a Calendar and will call `.deleteEvent` / `.listCalendars` / `.createEvent` on the Promise object. The function should either be made `async` and awaited inside, or the `getCalendar` call should be awaited before constructing the result object.

true_positives.matched_candidate:
enableSqlExpressions always returns false despite appearing conditional

The new `enableSqlExpressions` function reads:

```go
func enableSqlExpressions(h *ExpressionQueryReader) bool {
    enabled := !h.features.IsEnabledGlobally(featuremgmt.FlagSqlExpressions)
    if enabled {
        return false
    }
    return false
}
```

Both branches return `false`, so the function unconditionally disables SQL expressions regardless of `FlagSqlExpressions` state. The local `enabled` variable and the feature-flag lookup are entirely ineffective, and the parameter `h` is unused in any meaningful way. A future maintainer reading the function will reasonably assume `FlagSqlExpressions` controls the behavior and may be misled into thinking they can simply flip the `return false` to `return true` to re-enable the feature. Either replace the body with `return false` (and drop the now-unused parameter) or remove the function entirely and inline the constant, so the dead conditional does not suggest a configurable toggle that does not exist.

false_positives.candidate:
Event-pump goroutine in search.init panics on channel close

Inside `searchSupport.init`:
  go func() {
      for {
          v := <-events
          s.handleEvent(watchctx, v)
      }
  }()
The SQL backend closes `events` from `poller` when `b.done` fires (server Stop). After the channel is closed, `v` is a nil `*WrittenEvent`; the very next call to `s.handleEvent` does `evt.Type` and dereferences nil, panicking. The channel-close case must be handled, e.g. `for v := range events` (which terminates cleanly) or `for { v, ok := <-events; if !ok { return } ... }`. With this PR making init synchronous and more central to startup/shutdown, this latent panic on normal server Stop is more likely to be hit.

Postgres skip added — hides eager-init regression

The PR adds a hard skip for the postgres backend in this storage-server integration test:
  if dbType == "postgres" {
      t.Skip("skipping - test not working with postgres in Drone. Works locally.")
  }
The TODO comment makes it clear this is a new failure caused by this PR. Since the only behavior change in this PR that affects the storage server boot path is the move to eager initialization in NewResourceServer, the skip is most likely papering over a real regression in the eager-init path (e.g. an init that does not tolerate the postgres connection configuration used by this test, or a timeout when the postgres backend initializes). Hiding the failure with t.Skip means the regression is not caught in CI and is left to rot. Investigate the underlying cause (the test sets up postgres with a hardcoded `db_host=localhost`; verify whether NewResourceServer/Init blocks or fails in this configuration) and either fix the init path or fix the test config — do not merge with a skip.

